A||||7||rodmcneill||Diff. in 3.4 - 2.8||||5||
Z||000000||rodmcneill||01-19-2001||10:31 PM||||Besides the obvious displacement size difference what changes, improvements were made to the 3.4 motor over the 2.8? Was this motor avail. in any other GM product besides the Camaro and what years?||||reg||5
Z||000001||Raydar||01-19-2001||10:39 PM||||I think the 3.4 has an improved oiling system. It also was available in the Firebird. 93-96, I think. Same years as the Camaro. <BR>The same engine is also available as a crate engine from GM parts, for a (IMHO) bunch of money. The crate engine doesn't come with injectors, or other accessories, and drilling the holes for the starter will void the GM warranty. This is not to be confused with the aluminum head 3.4 which is widely available in most every mid-size FWD platform, but is a much more complicated swap, as none of the Fiero intake plumbing or ECM can be used. <P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>88 T-top Formula<BR>88 soon-to-be-V6 coupe<p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 01-19-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||fierosound||01-20-2001||02:42 AM||||You can use a 3.4L FWD engine, but you would only use the SHORT BLOCK.  Replace the pistons with 3.4L TDC pistons, and you can use the Fiero heads and every thing else.  Some of the newer FWD engines cam with a roller-cam setup, and of course already have the starter mounting bolts in the right location.  The only downside with a roller-cam engine is that thery is only one cam available from GM, and no aftermarket ones exist that I could find.  But with ported heads/intakes, headers and 1:6 rockers, the engine really moves!!!<BR> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/005508.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/005508.html</A> ||||reg||10
Z||000003||tgowens||01-20-2001||02:51 AM||||Rod, the 3.4 (rear wheel drive, push rod engine) we use for the 2.8L to 3.4L swaps comes from the '93-'95 Firebird or Camaro. There are 3.4L engines from the front wheel drive cars such as the Bonneville and Grand Prix which are far different than the ones we are using.<P>The 3.4 we use needs very little modification for the swap, mainly repositioning the starter and welding a stud on the forward engine strut bracket -- basicfally everything from the 2.8 bolts on. We <I>do</I>, however, use Lucas 305 injectors with an adjustable fuel pressure regulator to improve the performance. Result? more horsepower and more torque with no <I>visible</I> change at all -- ||||reg||1
Z||000004||fierosound||01-21-2001||01:38 PM||||Rod,<P>GM had 3.4L engines in many cars, but many had aluminium heads.  You would only be using the SHORT BLOCK, and would have to change pistons to utilized these engines. Camaros used the same iron heads as Fieros making the swap easier.  <P>Check these websites. You'll get a pretty good idea of what cars to check out at the wreckers with this information... <A HREF="http://members.aol.com/_ht_a/gpxss/index2.html" TARGET=_blank>http://members.aol.com/_ht_a/gpxss/index2.html</A>   <A HREF="http://members.aol.com/KWZ26/models.html" TARGET=_blank>http://members.aol.com/KWZ26/models.html</A>  <P>||||reg||10
Z||000005||ThatRickGuy||01-22-2001||10:23 AM||||The camero 3.4 is the exact same block/heads as the 2.8 except the cylinder side walls are thicker. The 3.4 has a longer crank (3.1) and to make up for the extra travel the piston rod is higer in the piston. ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Black88GT||01-22-2001||12:23 PM||||The 3.4 block is not the same block as the 2.8. The 3.1 is the same as the 2.8, maybe your getting them confused...<P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/cartmanico.gif">             <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/kennyico.gif">           <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/stanico.gif">         <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/kyleico.gif">             <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/chefico.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||mshill||01-22-2001||03:44 PM||||Black88GT,  no they are not identaical, but enough so that the 3.4 Pushrod is considered a "near" drop in replacement.||||reg||1
